# System-following palette through CSS

## Usage

A reader opens the site with a light system preference and gets a light canvas, dark text, purple MP accents and teal funder accents. Changing the system preference updates the open page without navigation. Existing callers retain their public interface.

```svelte
<Button variant="primary">Search declarations</Button>
<ResultRow class="funder-result">...</ResultRow>
<Notice tone="warning">Some amounts are unavailable.</Notice>
```

```css
.funder-result { --accent: var(--accent-funder); }
```

Components consume semantic tokens. Feature code selects entity identity through the existing `--accent` override. It never reads the current theme.

## Problem and grounding

`tokens.css` already owns the core palette, but `patterns.css`, `actions.css`, `styles.css`, `FunderSummary.svelte` and `PieChart.svelte` still contain literal appearance colors. Several badge and highlight mixes assume black backgrounds and white foregrounds. `base.css` and `app.html` force dark native controls. The document also hardcodes a black first-paint canvas and browser theme color. Changing only the core token block would leave visibly dark islands and weak light-theme contrast.

## Shape and ownership

Recommend a CSS-only palette, selected by `prefers-color-scheme`. `tokens.css` owns light and dark semantic color values. Existing dark values remain exact wherever possible. Theme is browser-owned state with two effective outcomes, light and dark. There is no persisted preference or JS state.

```css
:root {
  color-scheme: dark;
  --canvas: #000;
  --surface: #111;
  --text: #f0edf3;
  --accent-member: #b67be5;
  --accent-funder: #64cfb5;
  /* Remaining existing values and named roles live here. */
}
@media (prefers-color-scheme: light) {
  :root {
    color-scheme: light;
    --canvas: #faf9fc;
    --surface: #fff;
    --text: #241b2c;
    --accent-member: #7842a5;
    --accent-funder: #08745e;
    --action-ink: #fff;
    /* Contrast-tested values for every appearance role. */
  }
}
```

The shown light values are proposed inputs for visual and contrast validation, not measured results. Additional roles should express intent, such as field border, placeholder, elevated surface, row hover, chart remainder and subdued divider. Preserve existing dark literals through these roles, then give each role a light value. Share roles only when the usages have the same meaning.

`base.css` consumes canvas and text and stops declaring color-scheme. `patterns.css` and `actions.css` own component presentation. Their accent-derived mixes should combine the local `--accent` with palette-aware background or foreground endpoints. Keep these expressions on component selectors. Root-defined derived tokens can resolve against the root purple before a descendant overrides `--accent` to teal. Features keep ownership of chart geometry and domain identity; PieChart owns the neutral remainder appearance via a token.

There are no new runtime signatures or component props. The interface hides system preference, live updates and palette values behind existing custom properties. Reading the selected theme is unnecessary for the current CSS and SVG consumers.

## First paint

Update `app.html` before calling the feature complete. Its tiny inline fallback must use the same dark/default and light-media canvas policy as tokens. Advertise `light dark` in the color-scheme metadata and use media-qualified light/dark theme-color metadata. The fallback exists before Svelte hydration and must work with JavaScript disabled. This duplicates only initial canvas constants because the app template cannot directly import its compiled stylesheet; verify those constants against token values. Avoid a bootstrap script solely to set a theme attribute.

## Structurally distinct alternative

An explicit theme controller would own `type Theme = 'light' | 'dark'`, read `matchMedia`, set `document.documentElement.dataset.theme`, subscribe to changes and clean up the subscription. CSS would select `[data-theme]`. A bootstrap script before render would need the same resolution policy to prevent a flash, while SSR could not infer the preference without another boundary. This architecture centralizes a useful state API if non-CSS rendering needs theme values or a manual override becomes a real requirement. Today it exposes lifecycle and synchronization obligations while components still need the same token map. Reject it for the present scope.

A stylesheet-only `light-dark()` palette is another expression of the recommended shape, not a structurally independent architecture. Media blocks have clearer compatibility and preserve the present dark defaults.

## Migration and verification

1. Inventory every literal color, SVG fill and fixed black/white mix across the frontend. Classify each as theme appearance, entity/chart data color or intentionally fixed color.
2. Add semantic roles preserving existing dark appearance. Convert all theme-dependent consumers in the same change. Audit chart slice producers separately so unchanged categorical colors remain legible against the light surface.
3. Add the light palette and initial document fallback. Document that components inherit theme automatically and entity colors retain their meanings.
4. Run frontend checks and build. Exercise the design-system gallery in both browser-emulated color schemes, including fields, disabled controls, notices, focus rings, badges, charts, tooltips and empty/loading states.
5. Capture desktop search results, MP detail and funder detail in both themes. Compare dark screenshots against the pre-change baseline. Test preference changes while a page is open, then repeat an initial light navigation with JavaScript disabled. Measure normal text, muted text, accent text and primary-button contrast against their actual backgrounds.

## Tradeoffs and synthesis

Accept a small initial-document palette duplication in exchange for a correct pre-hydration canvas without runtime state. Keep this a reviewed and verified boundary. Recommend the CSS-media candidate as the base. No repository files were edited, and no visual or contrast results are claimed by this sketch.

The first implementation step is an appearance inventory followed by semantic token extraction with dark values unchanged.
# Light mode candidate

## Usage first

A reader with a light system preference opens search and immediately sees a light canvas, dark text, purple member identities and teal funder identities. Changing the system preference updates the current document without navigation. A reader with a dark preference retains the existing appearance. No switch, storage, hydration state or component theme props are introduced.

Existing callers remain the complete public interface.

```svelte
<Button variant="primary">Search</Button>
<Field type="date" label="Registered after" bind:value={registeredAfter} />
<div class="funder-page"><Badge>Funder</Badge></div>
```

Components consume semantic variables. The container's `--accent: var(--accent-funder)` continues to select the identity independently of the environment's light/dark choice.

## Problem and grounding

At ce97e29, `ui/styles.css` loads tokens, base, actions and patterns once through `+layout.svelte`. `tokens.css` owns the broad palette, but `base.css`, `app.html` and `Field.svelte` explicitly force dark native controls. `patterns.css`, `actions.css`, app `styles.css` and FunderSummary retain literal dark surfaces, borders and text. Several identity styles mix accents with literal black and white. Changing the seven broad tokens alone cannot produce a coherent light UI.

## Shape and ownership

Choose CSS media overrides with an explicit dark default. Keep color resolution entirely in the CSS cascade.

```css
/* Ownership sketch, declarations to implement after palette review. */
:root {
  color-scheme: dark;
  /* Existing dark values remain unchanged. */
  --canvas: /* existing */;
  --surface: /* existing */;
  --text: /* existing */;
  --accent-member: /* existing purple */;
  --accent-funder: /* existing teal */;
  --accent: var(--accent-member);
  /* Additional tokens own literal surfaces, lines, muted text and effects. */
}
@media (prefers-color-scheme: light) {
  :root {
    color-scheme: light;
    --canvas: /* pale neutral */;
    --surface: /* white or slightly tinted */;
    --text: /* dark neutral */;
    --accent-member: /* readable dark purple */;
    --accent-funder: /* readable dark teal */;
    --action-ink: /* light text for solid accent buttons */;
  }
}
```

`tokens.css` owns resolved palette values and the scheme. `base.css` consumes them without overriding the scheme. `actions.css` and `patterns.css` own component recipes and consume palette variables. `Field.svelte` inherits the root scheme. App `styles.css` owns page composition and consumes the same tokens. Feature chart adapters retain political party categories and their intentional colors; do not reinterpret those as entity accents.

Introduce semantic roles only for actual differences, including subtle action foreground/background/border and hover states, search border/placeholder/focus halo, tooltip surface/shadow, skeleton fills, identity fill/border/text, decoration, footer and selection. Preserve literal dark outcomes while migrating each declaration. For identity recipes, derive from the local accent and resolved palette; if a shared derivation changes dark output, retain an explicit dark recipe and supply its light counterpart centrally. Audit mixes with black/white as well as hex literals. Primary hover needs a light-specific blend direction so lightening a dark purple fill does not lose white-label contrast.

No runtime types or function signatures are needed. The existing semantic token names are the interface. Components receive no environment state and expose no theme choice.

`app.html` owns the pre-stylesheet paint boundary. Change color-scheme metadata to support both. Remove the inline fixed black background and rely on the supported scheme's native canvas until CSS arrives, or use a tiny media-aware inline background matching the palette if first-paint capture shows a mismatch. The latter intentionally duplicates only boot canvas values and requires a parity assertion. Replace fixed theme-color metadata with light/dark media-qualified entries matching the resolved canvas. Keep the root scheme authoritative after CSS loads.

## Alternatives considered

1. `light-dark()` values under `color-scheme: light dark` concentrate both palette values beside each role and remove the override block. This is a credible alternative with the same small component interface. It relies on browser support for every paired declaration, and the head's pre-paint styling still needs care. Existing forced dark declarations must still be removed. Prefer media overrides here because the existing dark defaults remain literal and reviewable, fallback behavior stays explicit and the design needs no per-subtree scheme switching.
2. A theme store with a root class adds synchronization between initial HTML, hydration, storage and system changes. It exposes application state for an environment setting and solves a manual-choice requirement the user has not requested. Reject it.

## Tradeoffs accepted

We accept a separate light token block in exchange for an unchanged and easily compared dark palette. We accept a few component-specific semantic roles where preserving existing dark details requires them. We avoid a general theme framework because the cascade already hides the entire environment adaptation behind the existing token interface.

## Verification and next step

First inventory and map every fixed palette declaration, then implement root palettes and migrate consumers in the same change. Use `/design-system` as the complete component specimen and real search, member, funder and registration-history views as integration checks. Test both schemes at desktop size, including mixed member/funder results, hover, focus, selection, badges, warning/empty/loading states, tooltips and charts. Verify readable normal and muted text, visible control boundaries and focus, primary labels and highlighted matches against their actual backgrounds. Test date fields and their native picker in both schemes. Change preference on a loaded page. The gallery reads computed swatch labels only on mount today; refresh those labels when the media query changes, or render paired static palette documentation so displayed labels cannot contradict the live colors. This is gallery presentation ownership, not application theme state. Capture a light reload before hydration, ideally with JavaScript disabled and slow CSS, to check the initial canvas. Compare dark screenshots to the baseline.

Run existing frontend check/build and relevant Playwright tests, adding behavior assertions for preference change, native scheme inheritance and any duplicated head canvas values. Capture desktop screenshots from the completed branch and attach them to the PR as required. No tests have been run for this design-only candidate.

## Synthesis and reconciliation

Candidate only. Parent orchestrator selects the base. No implementation deviations yet. Main remaining empirical risk is contrast in existing party chart colors and derived accent states on light surfaces; verify those directly before deciding whether additional chart presentation changes are required.
# Candidate: system-resolved theme state over shared semantic tokens

## Caller usage

The application has no theme control. It follows the visitor's operating-system preference, while all pages and shared UI components keep using the same semantic tokens.

```css
.panel { color: var(--text); background: var(--surface); }
.member-page { --accent: var(--accent-member); }
.funder-page { --accent: var(--accent-funder); }
```

The document exposes one resolved state, `data-theme="light"` or `data-theme="dark"`. The state selects a palette; it does not change page or entity identity.

## Data shape and ownership

```ts
type Theme = "light" | "dark";

function resolveTheme(prefersLight: boolean): Theme;
function applyTheme(root: HTMLElement, theme: Theme): void;
```

`resolveTheme` maps the browser's system preference to the finite theme vocabulary. The root document owns the applied state. The design system owns the token values in `frontend/src/lib/ui/tokens.css`; components consume semantic tokens and never branch on the theme. Keep `--accent-member` and `--accent-funder` as identity tokens, with `--accent` as the existing per-page alias. Keep party chart colors in their existing category palette, outside the surface theme palette, so a background change cannot recolor party identity.

The resolved state has only two values because there is no user override or manual switch in scope. If a switch is added later, model the preference separately as `"system" | "light" | "dark"` and derive `Theme`; do not conflate a stored preference with the active palette.

## Application shape

`frontend/src/lib/ui/tokens.css` defines the complete dark palette on `:root` and the light palette under `:root[data-theme="light"]`. Include surface, canvas, text, muted text, lines, warning, action ink, focus, and any remaining hard-coded surface colors as semantic tokens. `base.css` uses `color-scheme: light dark` only if supported consistently; preferably set `color-scheme: light` or `dark` from the selected root theme so native controls match.

A small browser-only resolver in `frontend/src/lib/theme.ts` reads `matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: light)")`, calls `applyTheme`, and listens for preference changes. Install it from `hooks.client.ts`, which already owns browser initialization. It writes the same root attribute idempotently. Do not persist anything because there is no user-selected preference.

## First paint and SSR

SSR cannot know the visitor's OS preference, so an explicit JS-resolved attribute risks a dark server render followed by a light repaint. To avoid a flash, a `data-theme` implementation needs a tiny synchronous script in `app.html` before the stylesheet is applied. It reads `matchMedia`, sets the root attribute, and lets CSS select the palette; SSR itself cannot know the OS preference. The client resolver then tracks preference changes. This is more machinery and creates a potential gap if the script is delayed or blocked. Do not try to combine a media query and selector in one selector list; CSS does not allow that. Supporting both CSS-only fallback and the explicit attribute means duplicating palette selectors or adding a build-time token generation step, so CSS-only is simpler when no script consumer needs the state.

## Tradeoff against CSS-only media queries

CSS-only `@media (prefers-color-scheme: light)` is the smallest and most reliable option for this exact requirement: it paints correctly before JavaScript, works with SSR, and handles OS changes automatically. It should be the implementation if no other code needs to observe the selected theme.

The explicit `data-theme` state gives scripts and diagnostics a canonical resolved value and leaves a clean extension point for a future override. It adds a browser resolver, an OS-change listener, and the risk of duplicated palette selectors or a flash if the CSS bootstrap is omitted. With no manual switch requested, that benefit is modest; preserve the explicit state only if another existing consumer needs to inspect theme. Otherwise use the same semantic tokens with CSS media queries alone and omit the TypeScript state layer.

## Verification

Use the existing `/design-system` gallery and representative landing, MP, funder, and party-chart views. In browser verification, emulate both `prefers-color-scheme` values and assert readable text, surfaces, borders, focus indicators, form controls, and chart labels. Confirm MP purple, funder teal, and party category colors remain stable across both themes. Check the initial HTML/CSS paint with JavaScript disabled, then enable JavaScript and change the emulated preference to verify no visible transition or stale state. Run the frontend check, production build, and affected Playwright tests when implementing.

